# OMA AI Cursor Orchestration

- Treat `@AGENTS.md` as the durable repository contract.
- Development OS Golden v1.0.0 cost/routing: `@docs/DEVELOPMENT_OS_COST_POLICY.md`, `@.cursor/rules/opencode-orchestrator.mdc`, `@.cursor/agents/routing-registry.json`. OpenCode `oma-coder` is default heavy; Cursor premium default **0**; no silent paid fallback.
- At the start of each request, state one mode: `SETUP_ONLY`, `SPEC_ONLY`, `IMPLEMENT`, `BUG`, or `REVIEW`.
- Read the constitution and smallest relevant spec before proposing functional changes.
- For product-shell, UI, interaction, motion, or visual work, also read `@docs/DESIGN_REFERENCE_STRATEGY.md`; do not plan the base design system until `DEC-013` is approved.
- During initial setup, create only repository/tooling/spec/workflow files. Do not create product architecture or product code.
- Do not plan or implement while a blocker decision changes repository ownership, identity/tenant authority, security, external spend, or the affected integration.
- Inspect actual files, versions, authentication, scripts, tests, and dirty state before installing or changing tools.
- Use built-in Explore for noisy repository discovery and built-in Browser for our own application.
- Do not perform browser-heavy external-reference investigation yourself. Delegate external reference products to the Reference Inspection Worker (`pnpm ref:inspect`) and reason over the artifacts in `.reference/`; `@docs/REFERENCE_PARITY_PROTOCOL.md` is canonical.
- Answer navigation, correctness, or reference-gap questions from generated parity artifacts, never from memory.
- Use `oma-spec-auditor` for independent scope/ownership review and `oma-verifier` for completion review.
- Start with zero subagents; use at most two for independent context-heavy work. Never fan out the same task.
- Use `inherit` models unless the user explicitly approves a pinned premium model/cost.
- Keep the parent accountable for handoffs, diff review, real checks, convergence, spec sync, and the final evidence report.
- Preserve OMA as an independent, original product under IMKAN. Do not copy IMKAN ONE or mix the visual systems of functional reference products.
- Never request or expose credentials in chat or files. Pause for the user's manual login when authentication is required.
- `CI_WAIT_AND_CONTINUE`: when the current authorized stage requires GitHub CI, do not end the task because a run is queued or in progress. After push, follow exactly one workflow run whose `headSha` equals the pushed HEAD. Stay in the same task until that run finishes. Prefer `gh run watch <run-id> --exit-status --interval 30`. If watch is unavailable or disconnects, poll `gh run view <run-id> --json status,conclusion,url,headSha` every 60 seconds and continue until completed. A shell wait during this period is expected. On success, continue to the next stage already authorized by the same command without asking for a new message. On failure, read the failed logs, identify the first causal error, reproduce it locally, fix it without weakening tests, commit, push, and wait on the new run. Maximum 3 CI repair cycles per requested stage; after the third failed corrected run, stop with a precise blocker. Rerun a workflow without a commit only when evidence shows an external GitHub infrastructure failure. Never trigger a duplicate workflow, weaken assertions, add skips or retries to hide failures, use an empty commit to trigger CI, continue after a failed required gate, or start an unauthorized product feature. If a run exceeds 45 minutes, inspect whether it is still progressing; keep waiting when logs show progress, and stop with `CI_STALLED` only when execution is no longer progressing. Authentication, MFA, and CAPTCHA remain a manual stop. This rule supersedes the previous return-immediately remote-monitoring rule. Blocking sleeps longer than 60 seconds remain forbidden; `gh run watch` is allowed for authorized CI continuation. Do not cancel a remote GitHub Actions run unless the user explicitly asks.
